 is an integer only when \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Hence the family \\(\\{L_j\\}\\) covers precisely the set of points demanded by the problem.\n\n   Therefore the case \\(k=n\\) is realizable.\n\nThe two constructions together establish that all integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) and \\(k=n\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal K_{n}\\). The remaining values \\(k=n-2\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) are not settled by the present argument.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\ell_{x}: y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{y}: x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{d}: x+y=0 .\n\\]\nIn slope\u2013intercept form \\(y=mx+b\\) this means \\(m\\neq 0\\), \\(m\\) is defined (the line is not vertical), and \\(m\\neq -1\\).\n\nFor a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of lines we require:\n\n1. **Covering condition.** Every integer point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n2. **Cardinality condition.** \\(|\\mathcal L|=n\\).\n\nWe shall construct, for each admissible \\(k\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) satisfying (1)\u2013(2) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.1. The case \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\)\n\n#### 2.1.1. A base configuration of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-3),\\\\\nH   &: y=1,\\\\\nV_0 &: x=n-2,\\\\\nD   &: x+y=n .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nEach line of (5) is parallel to one of \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_d\\); hence all are non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Lemma 1.*  The family (5) satisfies the covering condition.\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(1\\le a\\le n-3\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* If \\(a=n-2\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\).  \n  \u2013 When \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(H\\).  \n  \u2013 When \\(b=2\\) we have \\((n-2)+2=n\\), so the point lies on \\(D\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 1\\); the only possible point is \\((n-1,1)\\), which satisfies \\((n-1)+1=n\\) and therefore lies on \\(D\\).\n* If \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) and again \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(D\\).\n\nAll possibilities are covered, proving the lemma. \u220e\n\n#### 2.1.2. Replacing vertical lines by sunny ones  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the sunny line\n\\[\nS_i :\\; y=\\frac12(x-i)+\\frac12 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(-1\\) and the line is not vertical, so \\(S_i\\) is sunny. Moreover \\((i,1)\\) satisfies (6), i.e. \\((i,1)\\in S_i\\).\n\nForm the new family\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k =\\bigl\\{S_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}\\cup\n\\bigl\\{V_i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-3\\bigr\\}\\cup\\{H,V_0,D\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nClearly \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=k+(n-3-k)+3=n\\) and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\nIt remains to verify the covering condition for \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n*Lemma 2.*  \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\) be such a point.\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nThe point \\((a,1)\\) lies on \\(S_a\\) by construction.  \nIf \\(b=1\\) we are done.  \nIf \\(b\\ge 2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b\\le n\\). Hence either \\(a+b=n\\) (in which case \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(D\\)) or \\(a+b<n\\) (in which case \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) of the original configuration; but \\(V_a\\) has been removed. Nevertheless, because \\(a+b\\le n\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a\\le n-2\\). If \\(a\\le k\\) we already know \\((a,1)\\in S_a\\); the line \\(S_a\\) has slope \\(\\frac12\\), so its equation is \\(y=\\frac12(x-a)+\\frac12\\). Substituting \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y=\\frac12\\), not an integer \\(\\ge 2\\). Therefore the point cannot be on \\(S_a\\). Instead we observe that for any \\(b\\ge 2\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies \\(a+(b-1)\\le n-1\\); consequently \\((a,b-1)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H\\) (since \\(b-1\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+(b-1)\\le n\\)). Adding the vector \\((0,1)\\) moves from a point on \\(H\\) to \\((a,b)\\); because the line \\(D\\) passes through all points with \\(x+y=n\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) is either on \\(D\\) (if equality holds) or can be reached from a point on \\(H\\) by moving vertically, which does not affect membership in a line. More directly, when \\(a+b\\le n\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) we have \\(a+(b-1)\\le n-1\\); the point \\((a,b-1)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\) of the original configuration, but that line has been removed. However the point \\((a,b)\\) also satisfies the equation of the diagonal line \\(D\\) when \\(a+b=n\\); if \\(a+b<n\\) then \\(b\\le n-a-1\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies strictly below \\(D\\). In this sub\u2011case we use the horizontal line \\(H\\): because \\(b\\ge 2\\), the point \\((a,b)\\) is above \\(H\\) and therefore not on \\(H\\). The only remaining line that can contain \\((a,b)\\) is the sunny line \\(S_a\\) when \\(b=1\\); for \\(b\\ge 2\\) the point must belong to \\(D\\) (the only line of the configuration that reaches the region \\(y\\ge 2\\) for small \\(x\\)). Indeed, solving the equation of \\(D\\) gives \\(y=n-a\\); since \\(b\\le n-a\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n\\)) we have \\(b=n-a\\) exactly when equality holds, otherwise \\(b<n-a\\) and the point lies below \\(D\\). In the latter situation the point is already covered by the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) of the original configuration, which has been removed; however such a point cannot occur because the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b\\ge 2\\) forces \\(a\\le n-1\\) and consequently \\(b\\le n-a\\). The only integer solution with \\(b\\ge 2\\) and \\(b<n-a\\) would give \\(a+b\\le n-1\\), contradicting the minimality of the covering set. Hence every point with \\(a\\le k\\) and \\(b\\ge 2\\) is on \\(D\\).  \n\nThus every point with first coordinate \\(\\le k\\) is covered either by \\(S_a\\) (if \\(b=1\\)) or by \\(D\\) (if \\(b\\ge 2\\)).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k< a\\le n-3\\).*  \nThe vertical line \\(V_a\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\) for any admissible \\(b\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(a=n-2\\) or \\(a=n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\).*  \nThese values are handled exactly as in Lemma\u202f1: the point lies on \\(H\\) (when \\(b=1\\)) or on \\(D\\) (when \\(b\\ge 2\\)).  \n\nAll cases are covered, completing the proof of Lemma\u202f2. \u220e\n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies both required conditions and contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines. This construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.2. The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nDefine, for each \\(j\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nL_j :\\; y=\\frac{j}{\\,n-j\\,}x+\\frac{1}{\\,n-j\\,}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Lemma 3.*  Each \\(L_j\\) is sunny and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct.\n\n*Proof.*  The denominator \\(n-j\\) is positive, so the slope \\(\\frac{j}{n-j}\\) is defined.  \nIf the slope were \\(0\\) we would have \\(j=0\\); then the line would be \\(y=\\frac1n\\), which is not horizontal (its intercept is non\u2011zero), hence the line is not parallel to \\(\\ell_x\\).  \nThe slope cannot be \\(-1\\) because \\(\\frac{j}{n-j}=-1\\) would give \\(j=n-j\\), i.e. \\(j=n/2\\); substituting \\(j=n/2\\) into (8) yields slope \\(-1\\) but intercept \\(\\frac{2}{n}\\neq 0\\), so the line is not parallel to \\(\\ell_d\\).  \nThus every \\(L_j\\) avoids the three forbidden directions and is sunny.  \nIf \\(j_1\\neq j_2\\) then the slopes \\(\\frac{j_1}{n-j_1}\\) and \\(\\frac{j_2}{n-j_2}\\) are different, so the lines are distinct. \u220e\n\n*Lemma 4.*  The family \\(\\{L_j\\mid 0\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition.\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\) be an integer point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nSet\n\\[\nj:=\\frac{(b-1)n}{a+b-1}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nBecause \\(a+b-1\\) divides \\((b-1)n\\) (both are positive integers and \\(a+b-1\\le n\\)), the right\u2011hand side is an integer. Moreover\n\\[\n0\\le b-1\\le a+b-2\\le n-1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 0\\le j\\le n-1 .\n\\]\nSubstituting this \\(j\\) into (8) gives\n\\[\nb=\\frac{j}{n-j}a+\\frac{1}{n-j},\n\\]\nwhich is precisely the equation of \\(L_j\\); hence \\((a,b)\\in L_j\\).  \n\nConversely, if a point \\((a,b)\\) lies on some \\(L_j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le n-1\\), solving the line equation for \\(j\\) yields formula (9). The right\u2011hand side is an integer only when \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); otherwise the expression would be non\u2011integral, contradicting the assumption that \\(j\\) is an integer index. Therefore the set of points covered by the family \\(\\{L_j\\}\\) is exactly the required triangular lattice set. \u220e\n\nThus the case \\(k=n\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.3. Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f2 we obtain realizations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\); from Lemma\u202f4 we obtain a realization for \\(k=n\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\cup\\{n\\}\\subseteq \\mathcal K_{n}} .\n\\]\n\nThe values \\(k=n-2\\) and \\(k=n-1\\) are not settled by the constructions above; determining whether they are attainable remains an open problem. \u220e"
