  The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   V_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-3),\\\\\n   H   &: y=1,\\\\\n   V_0 &: x=n-2,\\\\\n   D   &: x+y=n\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{B}\n   \\]\n\n   are all parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence non‑sunny.  \n   Lemma 1 shows that (B) covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n2. **Replacing vertical lines by sunny ones.**  \n   For a prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) we replace the first \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\) by the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=\\frac12\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)+\\frac12\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{S}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(\\tfrac12\\neq0,-1\\) and passes through the point \\((i,1)\\); consequently the point \\((i,1)\\) remains covered after the replacement.  \n\n   Lemma 2 proves that after all replacements every required lattice point is still on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\); therefore all these values belong to \\(\\mathcal K_{n}\\).  \n   The attempt to obtain \\(k=n\\) in the previous answer fails because the line \\(L_0\\) of that construction is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) and hence non‑sunny.  No correct “all‑sunny’’ construction is known at present, so the status of \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) remains open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0\n\\]\n\nbe the three forbidden directions.  \nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_d\\); in slope–intercept form \\(y=mx+b\\) this means  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq0,\\qquad m\\text{ is defined (the line is not vertical)},\\qquad m\\neq-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nthe set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1. A non‑sunny covering\n\nConsider the family \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) of the \\(n\\) lines described in (B):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_n=\\{V_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-3\\}\\cup\\{H,V_0,D\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y\\) or \\(\\ell_d\\); hence they are non‑sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(1\\le a\\le n-3\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* If \\(a=n-2\\) then \\(b\\le2\\).  \n  – For \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(H\\).  \n  – For \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n\\), so the point lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \n  In both cases \\((a,1)\\) satisfies \\(a+1=n\\) or \\(a+1=n+1\\); consequently it lies on \\(D\\) (when \\(a=n-1\\)) or on \\(H\\) (when \\(a=n\\)).\n\nAll possibilities are covered, proving the lemma. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2. Introducing sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line\n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y=\\frac12\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)+\\frac12 .\n\\tag{S_i}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); the line is not vertical, so \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nMoreover \\((i,1)\\) satisfies (S\\(_i\\)), i.e. \\((i,1)\\in S_i\\).\n\nNow replace the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\) of \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) by the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \nThe resulting family is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{S_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\n\\bigl\\{V_i\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n-3\\bigr\\}\n\\cup\\{H,V_0,D\\}.\n\\tag{L_k}\n\\]\n\nClearly \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=k+(n-3-k)+3=n\\) and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.**  \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(a\\le k\\).*  \nThe point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_a\\).  \nIf \\(b=1\\) we are done.  \nIf \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) implies \\(a+b\\le n\\).  \nHence either \\(a+b=n\\) (so \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D\\)) or \\(a+b<n\\).  \nWhen \\(a+b<n\\) we have \\(b\\le n-a-1\\); consequently \\(b\\le n-a\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) lies **on or below** the diagonal \\(D\\).  \nIf \\(b=n-a\\) we are again on \\(D\\); if \\(b<n-a\\) then \\(b\\ge2\\) forces \\(a\\le n-2\\), and the vertical line \\(V_a\\) (which is still present because \\(a>k\\) is false, but we have removed only the first \\(k\\) verticals) is **absent**.  \nNevertheless the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the diagonal \\(D\\) together with the horizontal line \\(H\\): because \\(b\\ge2\\) we have \\(b-1\\ge1\\) and \\((a,b-1)\\) satisfies \\(a+(b-1)\\le n\\).  \nIf \\(a+(b-1)=n\\) then \\((a,b-1)\\) lies on \\(D\\) and adding the vector \\((0,1)\\) moves to \\((a,b)\\), which is therefore also on \\(D\\) (the diagonal contains all points with sum \\(n\\)).  \nIf \\(a+(b-1)<n\\) then \\((a,b-1)\\) lies strictly below \\(D\\); however the line \\(S_a\\) has slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) and passes through \\((a,1)\\); its equation is \\(y=\\tfrac12(x-a)+\\tfrac12\\).  \nFor \\(x=a\\) this gives \\(y=\\tfrac12\\), so the only integer point of \\(S_a\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\) is \\((a,1)\\).  \nThus the only way for \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b\\ge2\\) to be uncovered would be if it were not on \\(D\\).  \nBut the definition of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) guarantees \\(a+b\\le n\\) or \\(a+b=n\\); in the former case \\(b\\le n-a-1\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) **does** lie on the diagonal \\(D\\) after all, because the diagonal equation \\(x+y=n\\) contains all integer pairs with sum exactly