s answer is invalid because it produces a horizontal line (slope \\(0\\)), which is not sunny.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Base configuration of non‑sunny lines.**  \n   The \\(n\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   V_i &: x=i \\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,n-3),\\\\\n   H   &: y=1,\\\\\n   V_0 &: x=n-2,\\\\\n   D   &: x+y=n\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{B}\n   \\]\n\n   are all parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence non‑sunny.  \n   Lemma 1 shows that (B) covers every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n2. **Replacing vertical lines by sunny ones.**  \n   For a prescribed integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) we replace the first \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_k\\) by the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\; y=\\frac12\\bigl(x-i\\bigr)+\\frac12\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{S}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(S_i\\) has slope \\(\\tfrac12\\neq0,-1\\) and passes through the point \\((i,1)\\); consequently the point \\((i,1)\\) remains covered after the replacement.  \n\n   Lemma 2 proves that after all replacements every required lattice point is still on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines, and exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_i\\) are sunny.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\); therefore all these values belong to \\(\\mathcal K_{n}\\).  \n   The attempt to obtain \\(k=n\\) in the previous answer fails because the line \\(L_0\\) of that construction is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) and hence non‑sunny.  No correct “all‑sunny’’ construction is known at present, so the status of \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) remains open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0\n\\]\n\nbe the three forbidden directions.  \nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_d\\); in slope–intercept form 