\(b\\le n-a-1\\) and the point \\((a,b)\\) **does** lie on the diagonal \\(D\\) after all, because the diagonal equation \\(x+y=n\\) contains all integer pairs with sum exactly \\(n\\); the inequality \\(a+b<n\\) means the point is *below* the diagonal, but such a point is already covered by the vertical line \\(V_a\\) when \\(a>k\\).  \nSince here \\(a\\le k\\), the vertical line is missing; however the only points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le k\\) and second coordinate \\(\\ge2\\) are precisely those with \\(a+b=n\\) (otherwise \\(a+b\\le n-1\\) would give \\(b\\le n-a-1\\le n-k-2\\), contradicting \\(b\\ge2\\) for small \\(k\\)).  \nConsequently every \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le k\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) lies on \\(D\\).  \n\nThus all points with first coordinate \\(\\le k\\) are covered either by \\(S_a\\) (if \\(b=1\\)) or by \\(D\\) (if \\(b\\ge2\\)).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k<a\\le n-3\\).*  \nThe vertical line \\(V_a\\) is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\) for any admissible \\(b\\).\n\n*Case 3: \\(a=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\).*  \nExactly as in Lemma 1, such points are covered by \\(H\\) (when \\(b=1\\)) or by \\(D\\) (when \\(b\\ge2\\)).\n\nAll possibilities have been exhausted, so every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3. Consequences  \n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-3\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) constructed above consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, satisfies the covering condition, and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-3\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal K_{n}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4. What remains open  \n\nThe previous answer claimed that \\(k=n\\) is always attainable by the family  \n\n\\[\nL_j:\\; y=\\frac{j}{\\,n-j\\,}x+\\frac{1}{\\,n-j\\,}\\qquad(j=0,\\dots ,n-