in \\(k=n\\) in the previous answer fails because the line \\(L_0\\) of that construction is horizontal (slope \\(0\\)) and hence non‑sunny.  No correct “all‑sunny’’ construction is known at present, so the status of \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\) remains open.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_x:\\;y=0,\\qquad \\ell_y:\\;x=0,\\qquad \\ell_d:\\;x+y=0\n\\]\n\nbe the three forbidden directions.  \nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y,\\ell_d\\); in slope–intercept form \\(y=mx+b\\) this means  \n\n\\[\nm\\neq0,\\qquad m\\text{ is defined (the line is not vertical)},\\qquad m\\neq-1 .\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nthe set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1. A non‑sunny covering\n\nConsider the family \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) of the \\(n\\) lines described in (B):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B_n=\\{V_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-3\\}\\cup\\{H,V_0,D\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll of them are parallel to \\(\\ell_x,\\ell_y\\) or \\(\\ell_d\\); hence they are non‑sunny.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 1.**  \\(\\mathcal B_n\\) covers every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(1\\le a\\le n-3\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n* If \\(a=n-2\\) then \\(b\\le2\\).  \n  – For \\(b=1\\) the point lies on \\(H\\).  \n  – For \\(b=2\\) we have \\(a+b=n\\), so the point lies on the diagonal \\(D\\).\n* If \\(a=n-1\\) or \\(a=n\\) then \\(b=1\\) (otherwise \\(a+b>n+1\\)).  \n  In both cases \\((a,1)\\) satisfies \\(a+1=n\\) or \\(a+1=n+1\\); consequently it lies on \\(D\\) (when \\(a=n-1\\)) or on \\(H\\) (when \\(a=n\\)).\n\nAll possibilities are covered, proving the lemma. ∎\n\n\n\n------------------------------------------------------